-## Usage Examples
-
-### Basic Usage
-
-```bash
-# Scan a local codebase
-strix --target ./app-directory
-
-# Security review of a GitHub repository
-strix --target https://github.com/org/repo
-
-# Black-box web application assessment
-strix --target https://your-app.com
+# Headless, for CI and servers: quick scan, capped spend, fail on high or worse
+jitera-pentest -n --target ./ --scan-mode quick --max-budget 10 --fail-on high
 ```
 
-### API Testing (OpenAPI / Swagger / Postman)
+Scan modes are `quick`, `standard` and `deep` (the default). In headless mode the exit code is `0` when no findings were reported, `1` on a fatal error, and `2` when findings were reported (with `--fail-on`, only findings at or above that severity count).
 
-Point Strix at an API contract and it tests every declared endpoint instead of
-having to discover them by crawling. Pair the spec with the live base URL so the
-agent knows where to send traffic:
+### Results
 
-```bash
-# OpenAPI / Swagger file, Postman export, or a live collection by id
-strix --target ./openapi.yaml --target https://api.your-app.com
-strix --target postman://<collection-uuid> --target https://api.your-app.com
-```
+Each scan writes to `strix_runs/<run-name>/`:
 
+| File | Contents |
+|---|---|
+| `penetration_test_report.md` | The full report |
+| `vulnerabilities/*.md` | One file per finding |
+| `vulnerabilities.json` | All findings, machine-readable |
+| `findings.sarif` | SARIF 2.1.0 for code-scanning tools |
+| `run.json` | Status, timing and LLM cost |
 
-### Advanced Testing Scenarios
+Open them in the local viewer:
 
 ```bash
-# Grey-box authenticated testing
-strix --target https://your-app.com --instruction "Perform authenticated testing using credentials: user:pass"
-
-# Multi-target testing (source code + deployed app)
-strix -t https://github.com/org/app -t https://your-app.com
-
-# Targets from a file, one target per non-empty, non-comment line
-strix --target-list ./targets.txt
+jitera-pentest view                # most recent run
+jitera-pentest view my-run-name    # a specific run
 ```
 
-See the [CLI reference](https://docs.strix.ai/usage/cli) for every option, including scan modes, diff scope, instruction files, and budgets.
-
-### Headless Mode
-
-Run Strix programmatically without interactive UI using the `-n/--non-interactive` flag - perfect for servers and automated jobs. The CLI prints real-time vulnerability findings and the final report before exiting. Exits with non-zero code when vulnerabilities are found.
+### Other commands
 
 ```bash
-strix -n --target https://your-app.com
-```
-
-### CI/CD (GitHub Actions)
-
-Strix can be added to your pipeline to run a security test on pull requests with a lightweight GitHub Actions workflow:
-
-```yaml
-name: strix-penetration-test
-
-on:
-  pull_request:
-
-jobs:
-  security-scan:
-    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
-    steps:
-      - uses: actions/checkout@v6
-        with:
-          fetch-depth: 0
-
-      - name: Install Strix
-        run: curl -sSL https://strix.ai/install | bash
-
-      - name: Run Strix
-        env:
-          STRIX_LLM: ${{ secrets.STRIX_LLM }}
-          LLM_API_KEY: ${{ secrets.LLM_API_KEY }}
-
-        run: strix -n -t ./ --scan-mode quick
+jitera-pentest about                 # version and attribution
+jitera-pentest auth login chatgpt    # run on a ChatGPT subscription instead of an API key
+jitera-pentest --resume my-run-name  # continue a stopped scan
+jitera-pentest completions zsh       # shell completion (bash and fish too)
 ```
 
-> [!TIP]
-> In CI pull request runs, Strix automatically scopes quick reviews to changed files, which is why the
-> checkout above fetches full history. See the
-> [CI/CD documentation](https://docs.strix.ai/integrations/github-actions) for the details.
+## Configuration
 
-### Configuration
+Every upstream `STRIX_*` setting works. You can also write it as `JITERA_PENTEST_*`, and the `jitera-pentest` command copies it to the `STRIX_*` name.
 
-```bash
-export STRIX_LLM="vercel_ai_gateway/zai/glm-5.3"   # Vercel AI Gateway
-# export STRIX_LLM="openrouter/z-ai/glm-5.3"       # OpenRouter
-export LLM_API_KEY="your-api-key"
+| Variable | Purpose |
+|---|---|
+| `JITERA_PENTEST_LLM` | Model id, for example `openrouter/z-ai/glm-5.3` |
+| `LLM_API_KEY` | API key for that provider |
+| `LLM_API_BASE` | Base URL for a local model (Ollama, LM Studio) |
+| `PERPLEXITY_API_KEY` or `EXA_API_KEY` | Optional web search for the agents |
+| `JITERA_PENTEST_IMAGE` | Sandbox image. Default: upstream's `ghcr.io/usestrix/strix-sandbox:1.4.1` |
 
-# Optional
-export LLM_API_BASE="your-api-base-url"  # if using a local model, e.g. Ollama, LMStudio
-```
+Settings are saved in `~/.strix/cli-config.json`. To give the agents extra tools, list MCP servers in `~/.strix/mcp-servers.json`. The [upstream documentation](https://docs.strix.ai/) describes every option.
 
-> [!NOTE]
-> Strix automatically saves your configuration to `~/.strix/cli-config.json`, so you don't have to re-enter it on every run.
-> See the [configuration reference](https://docs.strix.ai/advanced/configuration) for every environment variable.
+## What's different from Strix
 
-#### Sign in with a ChatGPT subscription
+| | Strix | Jitera |
+|---|---|---|
+| Command | `strix` | `jitera-pentest` |
+| User-Agent of our own calls | `python-requests/…` | `Jitera/<version> (+https://jitera.com)` |
+| User-Agent of scan traffic | A generic Chrome browser | `Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; Jitera/<version>; +https://jitera.com)` on proxy replays, the sandbox browser, and requests the agent writes |
+| Telemetry | On, sent to Strix | Off by default |
+| `cloud` and `--update` | Strix Cloud and self-update from Strix releases | Blocked |
+| Viewer | Strix Cloud upsells, email and feedback relays | Hidden |
+| CLI, terminal UI, reports, viewer | Strix | Jitera |
 
-Instead of a metered API key, you can run Strix on your ChatGPT Plus/Pro subscription:
+Customers can recognize our authorized scan traffic by its User-Agent.
 
-```bash
-strix auth login chatgpt             # sign in with your ChatGPT account
-export STRIX_LLM="chatgpt/gpt-5.4"   # chatgpt/<model> runs on the subscription
-strix auth status                    # show the active sign-in, or logout to forget it
-```
+## Developing
 
-#### Use the managed platform: `strix cloud`
+This repository is a fork kept easy to sync with upstream:
 
-Run scans on [app.strix.ai](https://app.strix.ai) from the terminal, without Docker or an LLM key:
+- Edit the source directly. Our own code lives in `strix/jitera/`, our tests in `tests/jitera/`.
+- `fork/dev save` records every change since the upstream checkpoint (`fork/CHECKPOINT`) as patches in `fork/patches/`. Commit the code and its patches together.
+- `fork/dev sync` takes a new upstream: back to the checkpoint, remove our changes, take the latest upstream, re-apply the patches.
 
 ```bash
-strix cloud login                                  # browser sign-in, one credential per install
-strix cloud scans start --source . --yes --wait    # scan local code, approving the upload
-strix cloud scans start --engagement-type live_test --domain-ids <uuid> --wait
-strix cloud vulns list --severity critical
-```
-
-Every [REST API](https://docs.app.strix.ai) operation has a matching `strix cloud <resource> <verb>` command. Run `strix cloud` to list the resources, and add `help` to a resource to list its verbs. Output is JSON when stdout is not a terminal or when you pass `--json`. Binary downloads are the exception: redirect the raw bytes, or combine `--output FILE --json` for download metadata.
-
-See the [cloud CLI documentation](https://docs.strix.ai/cloud/cli) for scopes, workspaces, billing, and source-upload options.
-
-#### Connect your own MCP servers
-
-Strix can connect to Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers you list and expose their tools to the agent during a run. Create `~/.strix/mcp-servers.json` with a JSON list of local `stdio` servers or remote `http` servers:
-
-```json
-[
-  {
-    "name": "github",
-    "transport": "http",
-    "url": "https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/",
-    "auth": { "kind": "bearer", "token": "your-token" },
-    "allowed_tools": ["list_issues"]
-  }
-]
+fork/dev help       # every command
+fork/dev status     # checkpoint, how far upstream moved, patches out of date
+fork/dev test       # brand audit, lint, type check, tests
+fork/dev eval       # OWASP Juice Shop eval: did the scanner regress?
+fork/dev gepa       # optimize the agent's instructions with GEPA
 ```
 
-Each server's tools are namespaced by `name`, for example `github_list_issues`. See the [MCP documentation](https://docs.strix.ai/integrations/mcp) for the full schema, tool filtering, and `stdio` servers.
